---
import translations from "@utils/t.ts";
import Layout from "@layouts/Layout.astro";
import Container from "@src/components/Container.astro";

const t = translations(Astro.url);
---

<Layout title="Political Topics I Care About" description="A running list of the political topics I actually spend time thinking about.">
	<Container class="pt-16">
		<div class="text-center">
			<h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold">Political Topics I Care About</h1>
		</div>
		<div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
			<div class="text-center">
				<p>
					<a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/Andrew-Chen-Wang/blog/commits/main/src/pages/political-topics.astro">
						Edit History
					</a>
				</p>
			</div>
			<p>
				<i>Why is post-encouragement discouragement futile?</i>
			</p>

			<p set:html={t.political_topics_description} />

			<p>
				This is a living list. It's here so you know where I'm actually
				spending my thinking, and so I can point at it instead of
				re-explaining myself. For how I approach politics in
				general rather than any single topic, read
				<a href="/blog/2026-05-02-seesaw-politics/">My Practical Politics Is Everything But Neutral</a>.
			</p>

			<p>
				The lists are subdivided by personal beliefs, topics I care about, and topics
				I'm still learning. You can also read more of me being wrong, asking questions,
				and discussing on Reddit at u/pywang and
				<a href="https://toyourcredit.forum" target="_blank">ToYourCredit</a>.
			</p>

			<hr>

			<h2>
				Personal Beliefs
			</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Liberal democracies are the best for individual liberty but fragile and based on the idealism
					of its politicians.
				</li>
				<li>
					Energy: becoming oil independent through significant reduction in usage such as consumer electric
					vehicles. Increasing electricity for a more productive society through nuclear energy and other
					renewables without damaging the environment through landfills and overusage of space.
				</li>
				<li>
					Anti-war from an economic and moral perspective unless the cost of not waging it is extreme
					unhappiness, illiberalism, loss of liberty, and tons more deaths, i.e. Iran. But for the most
					part, anti-war.
				</li>
				<li>
					Politicians should be smarter: I think the modern politician is far more representative in the U.S.
					compared to most countries because the representatives are actual residents of the districts or
					states they represent. However, the original intent for politicians in America was for them to be
					educated. There is an intellectual elitism in the American left that believes college educated
					individuals would naturally be more leftist and that their policies are better, but simply having
					that degree does not make them more knowledgeable on political issues and solutions. Because of
					who is now entering politics, which over the last 80 years has been far more charismatic than
					earlier American politicians, I think the overall intellectualism, particularly in political
					theory, but also avoidance of populism has decreased. My favorite modern politician is Barack
					Obama. I've found interviews of him talking about the rise of China and him being anti-woke and
					purity tests dating back to 2019. He's made mistakes that I've criticized on online forums (tl;dr
					some of them: foreign (in)action in the Middle East, particularly the Arab Spring, starting
					to write flurries of executive orders that Trump continued in his first term and went wild in his
					second). But he was the best politician because he was practical while remaining ideological.
					Politics is slow moving, and people never appreciate that. It's why I say being a president is
					just a job. It's an honor to be one, but it's just a job. Or at least it's supposed to be with
					minimal corruption:/ Additionally, Obama staffed people who understood the nuance, but Mayor
					Mamdani is completing tasks that make him charismatic and to the Democratic base successful
					when his departments are also executing policies that pushes NYC's problems down the road
					like rent control. I still think Mamdani is a good, charismatic politician, but he chose
					people who are too ideological. President Trump has the same problem, though he doesn't mind it
					as much (probably because he doesn't care about America's long term future and is devoid of
					political theory and Democratic ideals, though I do believe in Susie Wiles when she says Trump
					is just trolling the left that he'll stay for a third term). President Trump's problem is that
					he has coalesced multiple factions, and people in those factions are now pushing their own agendas.
					The Christian faction was shunned out by President Reagan (and overseas, by Lee Kwan Yew), but
					it was necessary for Trump to install Christians who use their newfound power to push their beliefs
					against the public or take advantage of their base. Trump doesn't care about Christianity, and
					Christians know that. Mamdani is in the same position with executives installing rent control,
					which was a campaign promise, but again is doing harm against NYC. Obama hired the right talent
					that were laser focused on what needed to get done.
				</li>
				<li>
					Privacy and data ownership: I've written a few blogs on it and made a side project called
					SproutOS that deals with it. From a political perspective, that means ensuring the protection
					of individuals from an overpowered government that is finally (and now) put in the wrong hands
					that abuse the power outside of democratic ideals.
				</li>
				<li>
					Truth seek: this is a right-wing term, but it shouldn't be. It should be used by everyone to
					discover nuance. Of course, one can never believe everyone is willing to do the research and
					discover nuance. Understanding trade offs should be vital to those that are executing.
				</li>
				<li>
					Anti-woke and big tent politics: stop putting purity tests on everyone. Be tolerable of
					different peoples' beliefs and ethics. Society changes slowly. Socially shunning people
					turned into the biggest populist backlash because there was shunning of everything. I walked
					into college and got shunned for saying particular words or ideas. Thankfully, having a communist
					best friend at the time was great because he was a math major, so he had all the time in the world
					to talk. The purity tests are what made Democrats unlikable which costed them several election
					cycles and losing factions i.e. big tent politics. When Ezra Klein got cancelled for wanting big
					tent politics, it was crazy. Now, AOC is walking back Woke 1.0... so I guess times are shifting.
					I like how one person put it: left wing McCarthyism.
				</li>
				<li>
					The power of Congress and Electoral College: the legislative branch should restore its power against
					the executive in fear of Unitary Executive Theory. Both parties want it, and the media craves it,
					which means the common person craves it, too, because we love stories, heroes (especially if they're
					charismatic), and their journeys. American political theory is very interesting, and I love it.
					I think it's the perfect federal system for America given its history and even to this day nothing
					has really changed in nature. Leftists who hate the Electoral College and want everyone required
					to vote say that because it would mean Democrats would always win, but the point of representation
					is that people are representatives. Even during the founding of the country, people knew certain
					states would have too much leverage and too large of a population that would crush other states'
					representation. I started re-reading the Federalist Papers, and to think a bicameral legislature
					and Electoral College was not intended is dumb. The degree of influence may have changed (given
					all states ended up just voting via popular vote), and the Senate's filibuster gave too much power
					to the Senate, we have from an underlying theory an amazing political system compared to, say, a
					parliamentary system.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="/shortthoughts/2026-05-07-why-i-support-a-certain-form-of-dei/">DEI to a certain extent</a>
				</li>
			</ul>

			<hr>

			<h2>
				Topics I care about
			</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Reshoring jobs and protectionism in the fast paced global economy. I'm particularly inspired
					by Ohio (though they had a perfect opportunity to reshore given their monopsonistic towns),
					but, as a Michigander, I'm woefully disappointed by the state government in their inability
					to attract Americans back to Michigan. As I mentioned before, politicians need to be smarter.
					I care deeply about my home state and wish it could be revitalized, but country comes first.
					As the population in Michigan continues to age on average, the possibility of revitalization
					potential shrinks unless healthcare spurs economic growth which is possible; in which case,
					Michigan would need to invest in making it attractive cities because that's what the youth
					cares about, which then brings jobs.
				</li>
				<li>
					Universal healthcare: I do think universal healthcare is important, but it doesn't mean
					we have to adopt a single-payer system to do it. I'm a big advocate for incremental improvements.
					Slowly but surely, because that's just how politics was designed in America.
				</li>
				<li>
					Environmentalism, particularly protection of nature, the nuance of environmentalism and
					human productivity (and solutions to make both work out), the Great Lakes, forests, and national
					parks. I'm not a big hiker, but I appreciate being in nature, being surrounded by animals, letting
					animals bask in private, and having the privacy through being in forests / being connected to
					nature. Funny story: my dad enjoys the suburbs, and I have more and more friends love suburban
					living. My dad grew up on a tiny farm in China, and moved to the city in his adulthood. He hated
					the city, not just because of the sectionalism in China (that persists even today regarding white
					skin vs. tan skin which is the opposite of America), but because he is kind of a walking faux pas
					city-wise. There is a dichotomy between urbanists who are usually political and just casual people
					who want the space, tranquility, low cost of living, low stress, and low pollution of a suburb.
				</li>
				<li>
					The power of the individual: my favorite entertainment are Dune, both cinematic and book series,
					and Andor. I do think people need to struggle, but not suffer. I've written about this in a blog.
					However, what I haven't written about but is widely discussed is Dune's thinking on the power of
					an individual. Not just in the struggle, but politically as well with the Messiah figure, using
					the Voice in real life (metaphorically), etc. But religion will always be a thing. It won't ever
					die, and I think tolerance of differences should be learned.
				</li>
				<li>
					Appreciation of differences and selfishness: I'm a fan of YIMBYism but also rent control for
					certain zones of a city and for certain individuals to different extents, mostly elderly residents.
					I think there should be more understanding of nuance in issues.
					I think America is the best definition of tolerance of differences, though I'm worried about the
					rise of left wing violence as right wing violence has on long-term stayed still.
				</li>
				<li>
					Urbanism: I can understand the appeal and various factors of the appeal of suburbia. I personally
					like dense cities because (the analogy of gluten complexity incoming) it ensures ideas are flowed
					very quickly, gathers talent in a dense place, and highly efficient spaces. That said, I'm not
					anti-car, though cars should be EVs now (some things can't like a fighter jet, rocket, planes,
					huge industrial trucks, etc.). Cars have their place in the world.
				</li>
				<li>
					Getting political operatives with non-truth-seeking motives out: money in politics and rich
					people entering politics has been a thing forever. Secretary of State and democratic gubernatorial
					nominee in Michigan Jocelyn Benson is one of them, setting up her life to be the perfect spitting
					image of a politician. I met someone whose brother was the Harvard commencement speaker (and donors
					banned him from giving the HLS comment address too) who moved to Detroit to also set up a perfect
					story like being a public defender in preparation for entering politics. Both are from wealthy
					families, neither are from Michigan. It's the Obama playbook, but the playbook is decades old now.
					To be honest, I don't have that big of a problem with it if they live there long enough. Though
					I feel a patriotic sense to my home state, if they get the right policies in, then sure. However,
					I feel uncomfortable whenever politicians who advocate for certain policies loudly are not in the
					gray area. And that's what I see with the DSA and Republican Party. Everyone is a conservative
					to some degree; xenophobia is an example of this, and it's found in NYC (i.e. NYC transplants) and
					SF (i.e. tech companies). So I get it for conservatives because people don't like change because
					the future is unknown. However, I have a problem with folks who push an ideology without practical,
					historical within reasonable context, or academic backing, and this lack of nuance is the problem
					with the DSA when they push their narrative. Worse, DSA members are forcing people to believe
					that to accomplish their goals, they need to make all of America run under the socialist system.
					This to me is sinister. I have my thoughts on capitalism, reforms, and socialism from an innovation
					standpoint, and I've written about it before if you'd like to read.
				</li>
				<li>
					Anti-drug, pro health: being health is tough. It requires a good mindset, can be expensive food
					wise and tastes horrible depending on your goal, and requires the time to maintain that health.
					Drugs have caused tons of problems, even safe drugs that people want to legalize such as marijuana.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<hr>

			<h2>
				Topics I'm still learning about and are usually controversial
			</h2>

			<p>
				A quick list of things I'm still learning but haven't written a blog about my thoughts yet:
			</p>

			<ul>
				<li>
					Policy on immigration and fertility rate, and the unknown potential and degree of economic
					consequences society will burden for falling rates of either (skilled or unskilled regarding
					immigrants and education levels through culture and funding for children in America)
				</li>
				<li>
					Marxism: I'm politically intrigued in it. All of Marx's economic theories and political predictions
					failed, but the DSA is starting to thrive, so I've started reading into what the modern democratic
					socialist thinks should happen. I'm willing in the future to pay for a small socialist America city
					just to see if things would work out if the rest of the world is capitalist. However, a lot of
					the microeconomics don't work. The DSA
				</li>
				<li>
					Military spending, economics of war, and the degree of necessity to ensure global trade is
					peaceful.
				</li>
				<li>
					Land, Georgism, landlording (a la pied a terre tax in NYC), etc.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3>Market power and monopolization</h3>
			<p>
				Concentration is the thread running through most of what I
				write. Private markets are the right default, but past a
				certain size, capital stops competing and starts distorting:
				private equity rolling up mom-and-pop businesses, undercutting
				until the small competitor is gone, and platforms that resist
				change because the status quo pays them. See
				<a href="/blog/2026-05-04-what-business-is-too-powerful/">Power Imbalance and Leverage from Money's Perspective</a>,
				<a href="/blog/2024-08-15-solving-efficiency-against-monopolies/">Tackling Monopolies</a>, and
				<a href="/blog/2026-08-02-most-people-never-learn-enough-to-deserve-an-opinion/">Most people never learn enough to deserve opinion</a>.
			</p>

			<h3>Industrial policy and manufacturing</h3>
			<p>
				Whether a nation should make things, and which things. My
				position is high-value manufacturing, not assembly lines:
				chips and solar showed it can work when the talent base and
				product value line up. See
				<a href="/blog/2026-05-25-should-manufacturing-be-restored-in-america/">Should Manufacturing be Restored in America</a>.
			</p>

			<h3>Great power politics and defense</h3>
			<p>
				Balance as the stabilizing force in international relations,
				and what the actual constraints on military power look like
				once you follow the politics rather than the hardware. See
				<a href="/blog/2026-02-26-newtowns-third-law-for-international-politics/">Newton's Third Law for International Politics</a> and
				<a href="/blog/2026-04-23-how-americas-munition-stockpile-was-depleted/">How America's Munitions Stockpile was Depleted</a>.
			</p>

			<h3>Who pays for innovation</h3>
			<p>
				Public versus private funding, and what each is actually good
				at. Military budgets get credit for innovation mostly because
				they're concentrated; science funding is spread thin enough
				that breakthroughs get starved. See
				<a href="/blog/2026-07-01-military-vs-science-funding/">Military vs. Science Funding</a>.
			</p>

			<h3>Europe's trajectory</h3>
			<p>
				Whether Europe's slower growth is regulation, cultural choice,
				or both, and what it costs a bloc to opt out of competing. See
				<a href="/blog/2026-06-27-is-europe-poor/">Is Europe Poor or is Relaxation a Choice?</a>.
			</p>

			<h3>Opportunity, pipelines, and mobility</h3>
			<p>
				Most of what looks like merit is access to a funnel someone
				else told you about. Who gets into those funnels, and who
				never hears they exist, is a political question. See
				<a href="/blog/2026-07-01-pipelines/">Why Pipelines Matter in Life</a> and
				<a href="/blog/2026-07-01-rise-of-women/">Rise of Women</a>.
			</p>

			<h3>AI, labor, and what the panic is actually about</h3>
			<p>
				Less about AGI risk and more about who captures the gains, what
				gets built with the capital, and which fears are real versus
				marketed. See
				<a href="/blog/2024-10-26-agi-is-pointless/">AGI Is Pointless</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</Container>
</Layout>
